[{:db/id -1 :adr/id "ADR-2608133200" :adr/title "The retired binary was still being advertised: a live 401, a facade registry, and 20 READMEs" :adr/status "accepted" :adr/date "2026-08-13" :adr/supersedes "" :adr/superseded-by "" :adr/body "## Context

ADR-2607173000 retired babashka as this workspace's script host in favour of
nbb; ADR-2608131600 then measured what that conversion cost — 149 named tasks
unreachable across 28 of 35 converted repositories, 15 of them left with a
literally empty `{}` registry. That census also noted, in passing, that the
retired binary was still being ADVERTISED in three places. This ADR closes those
three, and reports two things the census did not know.

The distinction that organises all of it: a task that is absent from a registry
announces itself the moment you run it. A README, or a live 401, that names a
retired binary does not announce anything at all — it looks like an instruction.

## 1. cloud-murakumo's 401 named a second dead command

The census recorded that murakumo.cloud's 401 told callers to run
`bb murakumo token issue`. By the time this session looked, someone had already
changed it. It had NOT been fixed:

  before (measured live, 2026-08-13, POST https://murakumo.cloud/api/v1/messages)
    {\"error\":{\"type\":\"unauthorized\",\"message\":\"murakumo access token
     required — mint one with `clojure -M:token issue` (send as x-api-key or
     Bearer)\"}}

  $ clojure -M:token issue
    Execution error (ArityException) at cloud-murakumo.cli/-main (cli.cljc:341).
    Wrong number of args (1) passed to: cloud-murakumo.cli/cmd-token

`cmd-token` was declared `[action sub scope ttl-str]` — fixed arity — while
`-main` dispatches `(apply cmd-token args)` and the `:token` alias supplies only
`\"issue\"`. So the message had moved from one command that could not run to
another command that could not run. That is the worse of the two failures: the
first at least said `bb`, which a reader might recognise as retired.

**A working equivalent exists**, in two forms, and both were established by
running them rather than by reading them:

  clojure -M:token issue operator all 604800   -> mk1.eyJzdWIiOiJvcGVyYXRvciIs…
  nbb scripts/run-task.cljs token issue --sub operator --scope all --ttl 604800
      (in kotoba-lang/murakumo)                -> mk1.eyJzdWIiOiJvcGVyYXRvciIs…

The fix is not to rewrite the message to the four-argument spelling. The
optional-argument intent was already present in THREE places in the source — the
usage string reads `token issue [subject] [scope] [ttl-seconds]`, and the body
computes `(or sub \"operator\")`, `(or scope \"all\")` and a 2592000 default ttl.
Only the parameter list disagreed, which made those defaults unreachable. So the
defect is the arity, and correcting it makes the ALREADY-DEPLOYED message true.

Landed as network-awai/cloud-murakumo `ffc42ea` (`cmd-token` becomes
`[action & [sub scope ttl-str]]`). Verified by execution:

  issue                    -> sub=operator scope=all ttl=2592000
  issue laptop             -> sub=laptop   scope=all
  issue laptop chat        -> sub=laptop   scope=chat
  issue laptop chat 3600   -> exp - iat == 3600
  token verify             -> usage error preserved (action must be \"issue\")
  round trip               -> cloud-murakumo.token/verify — the SAME verifier the
                              Worker runs — accepts the CLI-minted token and
                              returns {:sub \"operator\", :scope \"all\", …}
  clojure -M:test -n cloud-murakumo.token-test -> 3 tests / 11 assertions, 0 fail

The same commit widens the 401 text to name WHERE to run it:

  after (in source, NOT yet deployed)
    \"murakumo access token required — mint one with `clojure -M:token issue
     [subject] [scope] [ttl-seconds]` in the cloud-murakumo checkout, or
     `nbb scripts/run-task.cljs token issue --scope chat` in kotoba-lang/murakumo;
     send it as x-api-key or Bearer\"

**I did not deploy.** Two reasons, in order of weight. First, it is unnecessary:
the arity fix makes the currently-live message true, so the user-facing defect is
gone with no production change at all. Second, verify-then-ship could not be
honoured cheaply — `npm run deploy` is `scripts/deploy-model-hosting-locked.mjs`,
which takes a moderator-scoped `MURAKUMO_MODEL_HOSTING_TOKEN`, acquires a
deployment lease against the live Worker, then runs a four-target shadow-cljs
release plus `clojure -M:site` before `wrangler deploy`. Shipping a
string-literal improvement through that, on a machine already at load 70–150, is
risk without benefit. The message text lands with whatever deploys next; the
half that mattered is live now. Both changed files were reader-checked
(`clojure.tools.reader`, 45 and 18 forms, clean) since no compile was run.

## 2. kuni-umi was a facade, and the facade was hiding two failures

`scripts/tasks.edn` read `{:test {:cmd [\"bb\" \"test\"]} :seed-dry-run {:cmd
[\"bb\" \"seed-dry-run\"]}}` while `bb.edn` was still present as the real
definition — the nbb registry shelling out to the binary ADR-2607173000 retired.
It worked on this laptop only because bb sits in ~/.local/bin, and would fail to
start on any murakumo fleet node.

Converting it properly surfaced two defects that bb had been MASKING, which is
the more interesting finding: a facade does not merely fail to convert, it
suppresses the evidence that conversion is needed.

  1. `seed-path` in `kotoba/ingest_mcp.cljc` walked four `.getParentFile` hops
     from `*file*`. bb sets `*file*` to an ABSOLUTE path, so those hops landed on
     the repo root. JVM Clojure sets it to the classpath-relative
     \"kuni_umi/kotoba/ingest_mcp.cljc\" — no `src` segment — so the third hop
     returned nil and the fourth threw at NAMESPACE LOAD time. That took the
     whole suite with it, because `ingest_mcp_test` requires the namespace: the
     failure was not in a test, it was in getting far enough to have tests. It
     now walks up and takes the first ancestor actually containing
     `kotoba/seed.edn`, so it does not encode the host's `*file*` convention.
  2. cheshire is BUNDLED in babashka, so `bb.edn` never declared it and two test
     namespaces simply required it. Declared in the `:test` alias.

Landed as cloud-itonami/kuni-umi `97a1dd4`. bb.edn removed; both
`#!/usr/bin/env bb` shebangs and the dead
`(= *file* (System/getProperty \"babashka.file\"))` self-exec hook removed.

Per-task verification — each run and its output read, not merely exit 0:

  nbb scripts/run-task.cljs test
    -> Ran 74 tests containing 532 assertions. 0 failures, 0 errors.
       Identical to the bb baseline measured BEFORE the conversion, which is the
       point of measuring the baseline first.
  nbb scripts/run-task.cljs seed-dry-run
    -> parsed 4 entities (~32 datoms) from seed.edn → com.etzhayyim.kuniumi
       DRY RUN — no writes.
       seed-path resolves to <repo>/kotoba/seed.edn
       still DRY with KOTOBA_TOKEN set, because `--dry-run` is passed explicitly
       rather than left to the default — so the task cannot silently become a
       live ingest if that default ever changes.

## 3. The README sweep, and exactly where it stops

Method: for each of the 41 converted checkouts, read README.md / CLAUDE.md /
AGENTS.md and `scripts/tasks.edn` from the repo's own `origin/main` — never the
local checkout, all 41 of which were stale. Every documented `bb <task>` was
checked for membership in that registry. Present -> the invocation is corrected.
Absent -> it is marked **unavailable**, with why it is a port rather than a
conversion and what still covers it. Nothing was deleted silently: a README that
drops a capability leaves the reader believing it was never there, which is the
same defect as item 1 wearing different clothes.

Where a replacement command is NAMED, it was run. That discipline paid twice:
org-ietf-ed25519's README offered `clojure -X:test` as the alternative to
`bb test`, and `-X` fails there (`No function found on command line or in
:exec-fn` — the alias supplies `:main-opts`); and its recorded transcript said
6 tests / 31 assertions when the suite is 11 / 71.

